Two images, because the MATLAB interface is judged by the route a user takes
rather than by the build.

Dockerfile-x86_64 is the builder: docker.io/mathworks/matlab-deps plus a C++/CUDA
toolchain and a MATLAB installed by mpm, MathWorks' package manager. The Jenkins
"matlab build" stage runs tools/ci/matlab-build.sh in it and produces the CPack
archive finufft-matlab-mex-linux.tar.gz.

Dockerfile-consume is the clean room: docker.io/mathworks/matlab, which already
carries a MATLAB, plus Parallel_Computing_Toolbox and the CUDA run-time shared
objects the MEX links against. No cmake, no CUDA toolkit and no FINUFFT file,
and the image asserts that at build time: without the assertion a stage that
quietly rebuilt or relinked would still pass. The "matlab consume" stage unpacks
the archive in it and runs the interface tests.

The base image does carry a gcc, because MATLAB bundles a compiler for its own
mex. Nothing in the interface tests invokes it.

The win, macpro and macm1 agents shipped with no MATLAB and no build toolchain,
so tools/ci/agent-provision.{sh,ps1} installs cmake, ninja and the same mpm
MATLAB into a cache beside the workspace, and on win the MSVC build tools as
well. That install needs administrator rights and is the one piece the script
cannot do for itself; it reports whether the agent holds them before it tries.

    podman build --file tools/matlab/docker/Dockerfile-x86_64 \
                 --build-arg MATLAB_RELEASE=R2025b \
                 --tag finufft-matlab:R2025b tools/matlab/docker

Installing MATLAB needs no license; starting it checks one out. Only the consume
half starts MATLAB, so only that half needs one:

    MLM_LICENSE_FILE=<port>@<host>   the institute network license manager

That is the variable `module load matlab` sets on the SCC hosts. Neither a
Kubernetes pod nor a provisioned agent has a module tree, so Jenkins supplies it
from a Secret text credential with id matlab-license, and tools/ci/matlab-consume.sh
fails with that instruction when it is missing.

A credential rather than a global environment variable because this Jenkins
console is readable without authentication, and Jenkins masks a credential in it.
The address of the license manager does not belong in a public build log.

Locally, a MATLAB that is already licensed on the host can stand in for both:

    podman run --rm --userns=keep-id \
               -v /usr/local/MATLAB:/usr/local/MATLAB:ro \
               -v "$HOME/.matlab:$HOME/.matlab:ro" -e HOME="$HOME" \
               -v $PWD:/io -w /io finufft-matlab:R2025b \
               env PATH=/usr/local/MATLAB/R2025b/bin:$PATH tools/ci/matlab-build.sh

The image carries CUDA so the same stage builds cufinufft_mex. Only the tests
need a device: tools/ci/matlab_test.m fails rather than skipping when
FINUFFT_CI_GPU=1 and no device answers.
